          "source_quote": "ἔσομαι δὲ οὐκ ὤν, οὐκ ὄντας εἰδώς, οὐ γινώσκων",
          "english_quote": "I shall be nonexistent, knowing those who do not exist, not knowing",
          "issue": "The draft's literal construction is grammatically possible: οὐκ ὄντας can be a substantival accusative object of εἰδώς. Nevertheless, its intended relationship to the surrounding catalogue, especially οὐ γινώσκων, remains unresolved. The note appropriately discloses this problem, but disclosure does not establish the intended meaning or justify removing the publication hold. This is an unresolved meaning problem, not a demonstrated transcription error; the supplied print verification should not be disregarded.",
          "suggested_revision": "Retain the supplied Greek and the explicitly qualified literal rendering pending a focused syntactic and contextual assessment of οὐκ ὄντας εἰδώς. Do not silently change the case, add a negation, or substitute wording from another edition. Any proposed emendation must be distinguished from the supplied reading and supported separately. No replacement is approved by this review.",
          "severity": "blocking"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "1.2",
      "verdict": "accept",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": true,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": []
    },
    {
      "reference": "1.3",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": false,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "εἴ ποτε οὖν ἐπεκράτει λόγος ὅτι ἀθάνατος, ἔχαιρον",
          "english_quote": "Whenever an argument that it was immortal prevailed, I rejoiced",
          "issue": "The inferential connection supplied by οὖν is omitted. The sentence applies the preceding account of alternating argumentative victories to Clement's emotional response.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use, for example, “So whenever an argument that it was immortal prevailed, I rejoiced.” This proposed wording requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἔτι μᾶλλον ἰλιγγίων ἐν τοῖς πράγμασιν. διὸ ἐκ τοῦ τῆς ψυχῆς βάθους ἐστέναζον",
          "english_quote": "and reeling still more amid these matters, I groaned from the depth of my soul",
          "issue": "ἰλιγγίων is a finite imperfect here, not the present participle represented by “reeling.” The draft folds the independent assertion into the following sentence and leaves the explicit consequence marked by διὸ unexpressed. The dizziness image survives, but the source's statement-and-consequence structure is weakened. Neither this form nor the supplied punctuation should be treated as an OCR defect.",
          "suggested_revision": "After the preceding recognition clause, use “I reeled still more amid these matters. That is why I groaned from the depth of my soul.” Review the reconstructed sentence afresh before acceptance.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἐμαυτῷ πυκνότερον ἡσυχάζειν ἐπιτάσσοντος",
          "english_quote": "when I kept ordering myself to be still",
          "issue": "“Kept ordering” expresses repetition but does not preserve the comparative πυκνότερον, “more frequently.”",
          "suggested_revision": "Use “when I more frequently ordered myself to be still,” or another readable formulation that explicitly retains the comparison. The revision is not yet accepted.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "1.4",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": false,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "Εἰ μήτι γε τοῦ νῦν με λυποῦντος ἐκεῖ χεῖρον παθεῖν ἔχω, μὴ βεβιωκὼς εὐσεβῶς",
          "english_quote": "What if, indeed, because I have not lived devoutly, I am to suffer there something worse than what troubles me now",
          "issue": "The apprehensive question is a defensible interpretation, but “What if” obscures the exceptive connection in Εἰ μήτι γε, more transparently “unless perhaps” or “unless indeed.” That connection qualifies the immediately preceding reassurance that present grief is superfluous. The lexical note gives the chosen interpretation without preserving this alternative.",
          "suggested_revision": "Explicitly compare “Unless, indeed, I am to suffer there something worse than what troubles me now, because I have not lived devoutly …” with the current apprehensive question. If “What if” is retained, disclose the exceptive alternative and explain the choice. Neither candidate is accepted without fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἀδήλου οὖν ὄντος τοῦ πράγματος (ἔλεγον) ἀκινδυνότερόν ἐστιν μᾶλλον εὐσεβῶς βιῶσαί με",
          "english_quote": "Since the matter is uncertain, then, it is safer for me to live devoutly instead.",
          "issue": "The parenthetical reporting clause (ἔλεγον), “I said,” is omitted. The earlier “And again I said” does not separately represent this repeated clause.",
          "suggested_revision": "Restore the parenthetical reporting clause, for example: “Since the matter is uncertain, then,” I said, “it is safer for me to live devoutly instead.” Check the resulting quotation boundaries and revised passage afresh.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
